.Dd July 1, 2026
.Dt XTC_PREEMPT 3
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm xtc_preempt_arm ,
.Nm xtc_preempt_disarm ,
.Nm xtc_preempt_supported ,
.Nm xtc_preempt_ticks ,
.Nm xtc_preempt_tick_pending
.Nd per-worker preemption timer seam
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In xtc_preempt.h
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_preempt_arm "int64_t interval_ns"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_preempt_disarm "void"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_preempt_supported "void"
.Ft uint64_t
.Fn xtc_preempt_ticks "void"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_preempt_tick_pending "void"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
These functions manage a per-thread preemption timer -- the seam the
libxtc preemption facility is built on (see the preemption design in the
project docs).  The timer measures the calling thread's own CPU time,
so a busy fiber accrues ticks while an idle worker does not.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_preempt_arm
arms a timer on the calling thread that fires every
.Fa interval_ns
of that thread's CPU time, delivering an internal signal to the thread.
Re-arming re-sets the interval.  It returns
.Dv XTC_OK ,
.Dv XTC_E_INVAL
for a non-positive interval, or
.Dv XTC_E_NOSYS
where per-thread CPU-time timers are unavailable.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_preempt_disarm
stops and deletes the calling thread's timer; it is safe to call when no
timer is armed.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_preempt_supported
returns non-zero where the platform provides per-thread CPU-time timers
(Linux, the BSDs, illumos, AIX) and zero otherwise, in which case
.Fn xtc_preempt_arm
returns
.Dv XTC_E_NOSYS .
.Pp
.Fn xtc_preempt_ticks
returns the total number of timer ticks the calling thread has observed
since arming -- telemetry, and the metric that confirms the seam works.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_preempt_tick_pending
returns non-zero if a tick has fired and is unconsumed, clearing the
flag.  The cooperative-assisted preemption path consults it at safe
points to decide whether to yield.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
The
.Ft int
functions return
.Dv XTC_OK
on success or a negative
.Dv XTC_E_*
code as described above.
.Sh NOTES
The timer only records ticks; it does not by itself preempt a running
fiber.  Higher layers of the preemption facility act on the ticks --
cooperatively (a yield at the next safe point) or, when the involuntary
path is enabled, via a signal-context involuntary yield.  Nothing arms
the timer unless a caller opts in, so the cooperative fast path is
unchanged when unused.
.Pp
The involuntary path (enabled by
.Fn xtc_preempt_set_involuntary )
is EXPERIMENTAL and off by default.  On x86-64 with the ucontext
coroutine substrate (the default build on glibc) it performs a true
resumable involuntary yield -- a fiber in a pure CPU loop with no
cooperative yield points is time-sliced so its loop-mates make progress
-- using an on-stack trampoline that the signal handler redirects the
interrupted instruction pointer to (Go's async-preemption method).  On
other substrates (the fcontext substrate used on musl, the Windows
fiber substrate) and in the single-file amalgamation it declines and
falls back to the cooperative path.  It is opt-in because a rare
livelock has been observed under pathological all-CPU-bound load;
untrusted pure-CPU work can alternatively run on
.Xr xtc_osproc 3 ,
an operating-system thread the kernel preempts.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr xtc_async 3 ,
.Xr xtc_exec 3 ,
.Xr xtc_osproc 3
.Sh AUTHORS
.An The XTC Project .
